WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.450
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.750
So in this lecture
and also the next one,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.060
we'll look at some of
the experimental findings

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.110
of neutrinos.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.390
Given the sheer
number of experiments

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.730
and the long history
in which we are

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.420
trying to understand neutrinos
and neutrinos' behavior,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.700
this can be rather confusing.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.400
So I'm trying to condense
this a little bit

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.740
and just give you the
highlights, or the basic pieces

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.670
of information.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.520
So this one here shows
a summary of what we

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.200
know from neutrino oscillation.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.930
So if we look at
atmospheric neutrinos,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.650
we find that mirror neutrinos
and anti-mirror neutrinos

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.810
disappear, and
they're most likely

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.940
converting into tau neutrinos
and anti-tau neutrinos.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.450
We look at accelerating
neutrinos-- here

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.015
we are using mirror
neutrinos and anti-mirror

00:00:55.015 --> 00:00:58.090
ones-- we can show that they
disappear over distances

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.460
of 200 to 800 kilometers.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.440
From accelerators, we also know
that they appear or reappear

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.810
as electron or
anti-electron neutrinos

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.370
over those same distances.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.560
From the solar
neutrinos, we know

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.010
that electron neutrinos
convert into mirror neutrinos

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.760
and/or tau neutrinos.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.700
There is more
detail to this story

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.860
than I'm giving
you here, where we

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.510
would have to discuss the
interaction of the matter

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.420
effects of neutrinos.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.010
That is for a different lecture.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.280
That goes beyond the scope of
what I want to discuss here.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.200
From reactor
neutrinos, we also know

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.620
that anti-electron
neutrinos disappear as well.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.310
So the name of the
game now is to take

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.740
all of those pieces
of information

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.270
and extract information about
the neutrinos' property.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:58.490
And in order to do that,
one has to make assumption

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.940
about the number of available
neutrino generations,

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.070
and in some part of
the interpretation,

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.487
also about the nature
of the neutrinos.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.580
As you can simply figure out
of the exercise we had before,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.640
it matters to the neutrino
oscillation probabilities

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.440
whether one assumes two or three
or four neutrinos in the mix.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.920
But if you just focus
here on three neutrinos,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.430
you still have the
problem that we have

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.570
degeneracies in the discussion.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:37.920
And they can be boiled down
to two major kind of trends.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.640
One is where the spectrum
of the neutrino mass

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.160
follows a normal
ordering, meaning

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.140
that the mass of
the first is smaller

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.790
than the mass of the
second is smaller

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.460
than the mass of the third, or
that the spectrum is inverted,

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.620
meaning that the mass of
the third might be smaller

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.240
than the mass of the
second and the mass of--

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.720
the first and the second.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.010
Data suggests that
the difference squared

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.040
in mass splittings
between those states

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.610
is such that delta
m12 squared is

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:16.610
much smaller than delta
m31 squared, which

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.700
is approximately the same
size as delta m32 squared.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.160
So if you look
now at the numbers

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.010
for the normal
hierarchy spectrum,

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.770
we find that the mass of the
first is much, much smaller

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.575
than the mass of the second,
which is a little bit smaller

00:03:32.575 --> 00:03:33.700
than the mass of the third.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.700
Numerically, we find
the mass of the second

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.710
is in the order of 8 times 10
to the minus 3 electron-volt,

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:46.630
and the mass of the third in the
order of 0.05 electron-volts,

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.040
so really, really small masses.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.190
The inverted spectrum-- here
the story is slightly different.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:59.310
Here we find that m1 is about
0.05 election-volts, which

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.590
is similar to the square root
of the mass splitting between 3

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:10.650
and 2, which is also
0.05 electron-volts.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.830
The information of the neutrino
oscillation experiment,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.670
and then how to map into
the individual parameters

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:22.350
of our neutrino CKM matrix
is summarized here, and also

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.420
in the mass information.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.520
And I don't want to
read the entire table.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.690
I'll just leave
this here for you.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.690
So in order to
understand this, one

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.730
has to go back to
the first slide

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.250
and understand what
kind of information

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.120
we extract from various neutrino
experiments-- for example,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.840
the solar neutrino experiments--
and then think about,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.990
is this sensitive
to oscillations

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.420
between the first and
the second generation,

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.270
or the first and the
third generation?

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:55.290
So that's kind of the mapping
you have to do in order

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.113
to understand this table fully.

00:04:57.113 --> 00:04:59.280
There are some experiment
where the information just

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.870
dominates the position
of a certain measurement.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:06.540
In others, there is combinations
of results coming out.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:12.240
The other reason why I put
this table here in this lecture

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.290
is to just illustrate
how diverse the landscape

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.660
of experiments is and
why that's needed.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.850
In order to get a full
picture of neutrinos

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.790
and their properties,
one has to identify

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.670
the individual properties
in the experiments

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.970
and then put the picture
back together in a global fit

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.990
or in a general
analysis of the data.

